The left figure (approximately x126–498, y24–951) is frontal and completely framed. The warm skin, mature facial detail, silver bob, relaxed presence and softly fuller clothed middle remain recognizably continuous with the original. The tucked ivory top, visible plum waistband, open caramel bands, two front patch pockets and complete oxblood low-heel shoes retain the intended look. This is qualitative visual continuity, not demographic certification or measured identity correspondence.

The center figure (x633–895, y23–944) presents a genuine page-left profile: the nose, head and clothed torso face left, and the chest–middle–seat contour supplies useful depth. Its complete near shoe and normally overlapping far shoe satisfy BRIEF.md:5; no missing footwear is inferred from permitted overlap. The right figure (x1067–1390, y23–945) presents the back of head and torso, both relaxed hands and complete feet/heels. Its quiet cardigan rear has no rear patch pockets. These orientations are visible in the figures, independently of their captions.

Across all three, the cardigan hems around y597–617 sit clearly above the plum skirt hems around y788–809, followed by exposed lower legs and complete modest footwear. Figure heights and floor placement are comparable. The profile and rear therefore add the combined body/layer/hem/leg/shoe relationship absent from the original front-plus-portrait and garment-only drawing. I found no dominant anatomy/garment artifact, cropping, youthful/thinned replacement or three-quarter substitution that defeats the full BRIEF.md:5 gate.

